24. Extracting variants of Interest
The final step after variant quality evaluation could be to filter large genomic datasets in order to find the most significant variants for experiment. This can be performed using “snpSift”. In the example below, we are extracting all the “missense variants” from the annotated VCF file.

$java -jar //mnt/d/RNA_NGS_Data/rice_India_SNP/snpEff/snpSift.jar filter "ANN[*].EFFECT = 'missense_variant'" /mnt/d/RNA_NGS_Data/rice_India_SNP/anno_snps-filter.vcf > /mnt/d/RNA_NGS_Data/rice_India_SNP/missense_snp.vcf

Note: If want to extract a set of chromosomal regions from the VCF file to be analyzed further,  use “tabix”. In this example, I am extracting all SNPs belonging to a region between genomic coordinates 30000000 and 40000000 of chromosome 1. Please note that to perform this step, the VCF file should be “bgzipped” and “tabixed” as shown in “Step 20”
$ tabix anno_snps-filter.vcf.gz chr1:30000000-40000000

25. To check the number of SNPs
 In each of the VCF files (raw VCF, filtered VCF, and annotated VCF), you can use a simple grep command to count the number of lines containing SNPs. Since VCF files follow a specific format, SNP records generally stBSY with a # symbol for headers or are data lines stBSYing with the chromosome (e.g., chr1, chr2, etc.). For SNPs, the INFO field will contain the details, including the type of variant, such as SNP

$grep -v "^#" raw_six_samples.vcf | grep -v "INDEL" | wc –l
$ grep -v "^#" raw_six_snps-filter.vcf | grep -v "INDEL" | wc –l
$ grep -v "^#" anno_snps-filter.vcf | grep -v "INDEL" | wc –l
$ grep -v "^#"  anno_snps-filter.vcf | wc -l
